## Why this project

Most RAG demos stop at "embed chunks, retrieve top-k, ask an LLM." Production systems fail in less obvious places: messy PDF layouts, chunks contaminated by neighboring content, concurrent ingestion corrupting a shared index, rate limits, silent retrieval regressions, and answers with no grounding.

PaperTrail is built to address those problems directly:

- **Evaluation first.** A golden set and a baseline exist before any optimization.
- **Layout-aware ingestion.** Papers are parsed by structure (sections, tables, captions), not split blindly by character count.
- **Hybrid retrieval.** Dense and sparse search are fused and reranked, with each stage ablated.
- **Grounded answers.** Citations are enforced in the output schema, and the system abstains on weak evidence.
- **Service-grade engineering.** Tenant isolation, tracing, timeouts, retries, tests and CI.

## What it does

- Ingests PDFs per tenant through an idempotent, asynchronous pipeline.
- Parses documents with structure awareness and attaches metadata to every chunk (paper, section, page, element type).
- Answers natural-language questions with citations that point to a paper and page.
- Routes questions: content questions go to hybrid retrieval, and metadata questions (year, author, venue) go to a text-to-SQL path.
- Abstains with an explanation when evidence is insufficient.
- Isolates tenants so one tenant's documents are never retrievable by another.
- Streams responses and records traces (latency, token usage, cost) for every query.

## Architecture

```
                         ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  PDFs ──► Docling parse ──► structure-aware chunking                  │
                         │        │                                   │
                         │        ▼                                   │
                         │  embed (dense + sparse)                    │
                         │        │                                   │
                         │        ▼                                   │
                         │  Qdrant (tenant-filtered)   Postgres       │
                         │                             (papers, jobs) │
                         └────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Query ──► auth / tenant ──► router (LangGraph)
                                 │
              ┌──────────────────┼───────────────────┐
              ▼                  ▼                   ▼
        vector path        metadata path         abstain
   rewrite → hybrid →     text-to-SQL over      (insufficient
   RRF → rerank           papers table           evidence)
              └──────────────────┬───────────────────┘
                                 ▼
                    synthesizer (citations enforced)
                                 ▼
                           SSE stream response

  Cross-cutting: Langfuse tracing · structured logging · retries/timeouts · eval harness in CI
```

### Components

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| **Ingestion** | Parse PDFs, chunk by structure, embed, upsert idempotently via content hashing, track job state |
| **Retrieval** | Dense + sparse search, Reciprocal Rank Fusion, cross-encoder reranking, tenant filtering |
| **Agent layer** | Small LangGraph workflow: router, vector path, metadata (SQL) path, synthesizer |
| **LLM wrapper** | Single module for all LLM calls: timeouts, capped retries with backoff, token and cost accounting |
| **Evaluation** | Golden-set loader, retrieval and answer metrics, ablation runner, CI regression gate |
| **Observability** | Traces, structured logs, per-query latency and cost |

## Tech stack

| Area | Tools |
|---|---|
| Language and API | Python, FastAPI, Pydantic v2 |
| Document parsing | Docling (PyMuPDF as fallback) |
| Vector store | Qdrant (dense + sparse vectors, payload filters for tenancy) |
| Embeddings and reranking | BGE-family embedding model (e.g. bge-m3 / bge-small), BGE cross-encoder reranker |
| Orchestration | LangGraph (small, deliberate graph) |
| LLM | A cost-efficient mini-tier model via API (configurable) |
| Relational storage | PostgreSQL (paper metadata, ingestion jobs) |
| Evaluation | Custom golden set + metrics; RAGAS / DeepEval as secondary checks |
| Observability | Langfuse, structured logging |
| Testing and quality | pytest, Ruff |
| Packaging and CI | Docker, Docker Compose, GitHub Actions |
| Deployment | Fly.io / Render / single VM *(planned)* |

## Dataset

**Corpus:** a focused collection of roughly 100–150 open-access research papers from [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/), chosen around one narrow topic (for example, retrieval-augmented generation and LLM evaluation) so that questions are answerable and multi-hop questions are possible.

**Licensing and redistribution:**
- arXiv metadata is openly available, but each paper carries its own license. Only papers with licenses permitting reuse are used.
- PDFs are **not committed** to this repository. A script downloads the corpus from a list of arXiv IDs (`data/corpus_manifest.csv`).

**Golden evaluation set:** 40–60 hand-written question/answer pairs, each annotated with the source paper and page(s). Question types:

| Type | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Factual lookup | Tests basic retrieval and extraction |
| Multi-hop | Needs evidence from more than one chunk or paper |
| Table / figure-related | Tests structure-aware parsing |
| Metadata | Tests the SQL path (year, author, venue) |
| Unanswerable | Tests abstention and hallucination resistance |

The golden set is small and committed under `data/eval/`.

## Evaluation

Evaluation is the backbone of the project. Every change is measured against the same golden set.

**Retrieval metrics:** Recall@k, MRR, nDCG.
**Answer metrics:** answer correctness, faithfulness to retrieved context, citation accuracy, abstention accuracy on unanswerable questions.
**System metrics:** p50/p95 latency, tokens and cost per query, ingestion throughput.

**Planned ablation ladder:**

1. Naive baseline (fixed-size chunks, dense-only, top-k)
2. + Structure-aware chunking
3. + Hybrid (dense + sparse) with RRF
4. + Cross-encoder reranking
5. + Query rewriting
6. + Router and abstention

LLM-judged metrics are spot-checked by hand rather than trusted blindly. A reduced version of the evaluation runs in CI as a regression gate.

## Project structure

```
papertrail/
├── README.md
├── pyproject.toml            # dependencies, ruff/pytest config
├── Makefile                  # make run / test / eval / lint
├── .env.example              # every env var, no secrets
├── .gitignore
├── Dockerfile
├── docker-compose.yml        # app + qdrant + postgres (+ langfuse)
├── .github/workflows/ci.yml  # lint, tests, small eval gate
│
├── configs/                  # chunk sizes, model names, retrieval params (yaml)
├── prompts/                  # versioned prompt files, not inline strings
│
├── docs/
│   ├── architecture.md
│   ├── decisions/            # ADRs, e.g. 001-vector-store.md
│   ├── failure-modes.md      # symptom → root cause → fix → test
│   └── results.md            # ablation tables, latency, cost
│
├── data/                     # gitignored except eval/
│   ├── raw/
│   ├── processed/
│   ├── corpus_manifest.csv   # arXiv IDs used
│   └── eval/                 # golden set (committed)
│
├── notebooks/                # exploration only; no logic lives here
├── scripts/                  # CLI entrypoints: ingest_corpus.py, run_eval.py
│
├── src/papertrail/
│   ├── api/                  # main.py, routes/, deps.py, schemas
│   ├── core/                 # config, logging, errors, auth
│   ├── ingestion/            # parsing, chunking, pipeline, jobs
│   ├── retrieval/            # embeddings, vector store client, hybrid, rerank
│   ├── agents/               # graph, nodes, state
│   ├── llm/                  # client wrapper: retries, timeouts, token accounting
│   ├── db/                   # models, repositories, migrations
│   ├── evaluation/           # metrics, runner, dataset loading
│   └── observability/        # tracing setup, metrics
│
└── tests/
    ├── unit/
    ├── integration/          # needs qdrant/postgres via compose
    └── eval/                 # regression checks on the golden set
```

### Conventions

- **Thin API layer.** Routes validate input and call services; no business logic in routes.
- **Dependencies point inward.** `api` may import `retrieval` and `agents`, never the reverse.
- **One wrapper per external system.** LLM, vector store and database each live in a single module so they can be swapped or mocked.
- **Config in one place.** Nothing outside `core/config.py` reads environment variables.
- **Notebooks are disposable.** Anything worth keeping moves into `src/`.

## Getting started

*(planned — commands will be finalized as the project is built)*

**Prerequisites:** Python 3.11+, Docker and Docker Compose, an LLM API key.

```bash
# 1. Clone
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/papertrail.git
cd papertrail

# 2. Configure
cp .env.example .env        # fill in API keys and settings

# 3. Start dependencies (Qdrant, Postgres, Langfuse)
docker compose up -d

# 4. Install
pip install -e ".[dev]"

# 5. Download the corpus and ingest it
python scripts/ingest_corpus.py --manifest data/corpus_manifest.csv --tenant demo

# 6. Run the API
make run

# 7. Run tests and evaluation
make test
make eval
```

## Configuration

All settings are loaded through a single settings module and documented in `.env.example`. Expected groups:

| Group | Examples |
|---|---|
| LLM | API key, model name, request timeout, max retries, concurrency limit |
| Qdrant | URL, collection naming, vector dimensions |
| Postgres | connection URL |
| Retrieval | top-k, fusion parameters, reranker model, abstention threshold |
| Observability | Langfuse keys, log level |
| Auth | API-key to tenant mapping |

Retrieval and chunking parameters live in `configs/` so experiments are reproducible.

## API

*(planned design)*

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/v1/documents` | Upload a PDF for the authenticated tenant; returns a job ID |
| `GET` | `/v1/jobs/{job_id}` | Ingestion job status |
| `POST` | `/v1/query` | Ask a question; streams an answer with citations |
| `GET` | `/healthz` | Liveness and dependency checks |

Authentication uses a simple API key that maps to a tenant. Every query and ingestion call is scoped to that tenant.

**Example response shape (planned):**

```json
{
  "answer": "...",
  "citations": [
    { "paper_id": "...", "title": "...", "page": 7, "snippet_id": "..." }
  ],
  "abstained": false,
  "route": "vector"
}
```

## Roadmap

| Day | Focus | Deliverable |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Scope, corpus, baseline | Golden set + naive baseline numbers |
| 2 | Ingestion | Structure-aware parsing and chunking, idempotent jobs, tenant isolation test |
| 3 | Retrieval | Hybrid + rerank, ablation table |
| 4 | Agent layer | Router, SQL path, citations, abstention, streaming |
| 5 | Production hardening | Tracing, retries/timeouts, auth tests, CI eval gate, Docker |
| 6 | Ship | Deployment, README polish, demo recording |

Progress checklist:

- [ ] Corpus selected and manifest committed
- [ ] Golden set written
- [ ] Naive baseline measured
- [ ] Ingestion pipeline
- [ ] Hybrid retrieval and reranking
- [ ] Router, SQL path, abstention
- [ ] Observability and hardening
- [ ] CI with eval gate
- [ ] Deployed demo

## Design decisions

Short architecture decision records live in `docs/decisions/`. Planned records include:

- Why Qdrant (dense + sparse in one store, payload filtering for tenancy)
- Chunking strategy and chunk-size choice
- Why hybrid retrieval with RRF instead of score blending
- Why a small LangGraph workflow instead of a large agent
- Tenant isolation approach (payload filters vs. separate collections)
- How abstention thresholds are chosen

## Failure modes

`docs/failure-modes.md` documents real problems encountered while building and the engineering fixes, each as **symptom → root cause → fix → regression test**. The areas it covers include:

- Cross-document contamination from concurrent writes to shared state
- Parser or vision-model hallucination on sparse or unusual regions
- Pathological regexes that hang on large documents
- LLM rate-limit handling (concurrency limits, capped retries, timeouts)
- Silent retrieval regressions caught by the eval gate

